{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And you shall bless YHWH your Elohim.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): when you remember the days of evil that befell you in the exile of Egypt, and your being there in great servitude, and you also remember the affliction of the wilderness now — when you come into the land and eat and are sated from its fruit and from its goodness, you shall bless for this to the name of the Holy One, blessed be He." "<b>And.</b> According to the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory, 'and you shall bless YHWH your Elohim' is a positive commandment to recite the blessing after the meal (birkat ha-mazon), and it is like (Deuteronomy 22:8) ״ועשית מעקה לגגך״, (Deuteronomy 16:1) ״ועשית פסח לה' אלהיך״. The phrase 'and' explains 'upon the land,' and 'upon the land.'" "<b>And you should be roused</b> to ask here, for in this verse there is a gateway to understand the secret of the blessings. And you will not find in all the Torah in any place that the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded us to bless His name except in this place. From this David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 145:1) ״ואברכה שמך״, and said (Psalms 100:4) ״הודו לו ברכו שמו״, and many like these." "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat)</b> say first, that the blessings are not a need of the Most High but a need of the lowly, for since He, blessed be He, is the source of blessing and all blessings cascade from Him, all created beings who bless Him — none of their blessings are worthy of Him, for He is the First Being, who brought all beings into existence, and their existence is naught but His existence, and His existence suffices in itself, needing nothing outside itself. If so, even if they were to bless Him all day and all night, what would thereby increase, or what would they give to Him, or what would He take from their hand? The benefit and the increase are only for us, for everyone who blesses over something from which he derives benefit testifies to the providence — that He, exalted be He, provides sustenance for the lowly so that they may live, and through their merit the grain and the fruits are blessed and multiplied. And one who derives benefit without blessing robs Him of the providence and hands over the governance of the lowly to the stars and constellations. And this is what they said: 'Anyone who derives benefit from this world without a blessing is as though he robs the Holy One, blessed be He, and Knesset Israel,' as it is said (Proverbs 28:24) ״גוזל אביו ואמו״ — that is, he robs the Holy One, blessed be He, of the providence, and Knesset Israel of the fruits, for through this sin the fruits are diminished for Israel on his account, since the entire world is judged according to its majority and each individual is judged according to his majority; therefore one must show himself as if the entire world were hanging upon him." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah</b>, 'and you shall bless YHWH' — the blessing is not merely a need of the lowly, nor is it only an expression of thanksgiving, but it is an expression of addition and increase, from the language of (Exodus 23:25) ״וברך את לחמך ואת מימיך״." "<b>And you should consider</b> the saying of our Rabbis of blessed memory, which they stated in Bava Metzia, chapter HaMekabel, at its end, in the matter of a creditor: (Deuteronomy 24:13) ״ושכב בשלמתו וברכך״ — 'and he shall sleep in his garment and bless you' — one who needs blessing, that is, the ordinary person; the sacred offering is excluded, for it does not need blessing. And the Gemara raises an objection there: But is it not so, and is it not written ״ואכלת ושבעת וברכת את ה' אלהיך״? Rather, ״ולך תהיה צדקה״ — 'and it shall be righteousness for you' — the sacred offering is excluded, for it does not need charity, since all acts of righteousness are His. The Sages of blessed memory explained explicitly that the sacred offering does need blessing but does not need charity, and it is explicitly stated in Berakhot: he said to him, 'Yishmael my son, bless me,' and so in the tractate Shabbat the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses, 'It was for you to help Me,' and that is the matter of blessing. And so when we bless the Holy One, blessed be He, there is in this an addition of the spirit of prophecy, and our blessing is a cause that all His creations are blessed from Him. And they further said that the Holy One, blessed be He, longs for the prayer of the righteous, and if the blessing and the prayer were nothing but thanksgiving alone, why would He long for the prayer of the righteous who are fewer than all the other numerous nations — and yet 'in the multitude of people is the king's glory' (Proverbs 14:28). But the expression וברכת connotes addition and increase, and it is from the language of a spring (ברכה) welling up from its source, and therefore we mention in our prayer תתברך תתרומם — 'may You be blessed, may You be exalted' — and likewise in the language of the Kaddish: יתברך וישתבח ויתפאר ויתרומם ויתנשא — 'may He be blessed and praised and glorified and exalted and uplifted.' And furthermore, from the language of בריכה and כריעה, bending the knee — one before whom every knee bends; and so we find in Sefer HaBahir: What indicates that this word 'blessing' (ברכה) is the language of 'knee' (ברך)? As it is said (Isaiah 45:23) ״כי לי תכרע כל ברך״ — 'for to Me shall every knee bend' — one before whom every knee bends."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {1 "The final clause 'ובאור על הארץ ועל הארץ' is opaque; read hyper-literally. The repetition of 'על הארץ' likely refers to the two occurrences of the word in the verse, but the syntax as printed is difficult."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:17.531396772Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}